 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "The highest railway on Earth runs on ground that must never melt.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The Qinghai-Tibet line in China crosses about 550 kilometers of permafrost.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's soil frozen solid all year, as hard as concrete.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Here's the problem. A railway embankment is dark, and it soaks up the sun.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The heat sinks into the ground, and the ice under the tracks starts to melt.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Melted permafrost turns into mud, and the tracks sink unevenly.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A train at 100 km/h can't survive a dip like that.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the engineers did something clever. They keep the ground cold on purpose.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "They built the embankment from big loose rocks, so cold winter air sinks deep into the gaps.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Along the line stand thousands of metal pipes, like giant straws in the ground.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Inside, a liquid boils with ground heat, rises, cools in the wind, and drips back down.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "No pump, no power. They just move heat out of the soil all winter.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And where the ice is worst, the track runs on bridges instead of soil.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A railway built to protect the cold underneath it. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.2
  }
 ],
